@begin(section)

@title(A Longer Example)

The quickstart showed the whole loop.  This page works through a
realistic scenario end to end: a small project that needs two binary
dependencies, set up on a dev machine and then reproduced in CI — and,
at the end, the same project using the dependency resolver to choose
the versions.

Imagine a project, @c(wallaby), that needs a prebuilt command-line tool
(@c(wally)) and a data pack (@c(parks-data)) to build and run.

@begin(section)
@title(On a Dev Machine)

@begin(section)
@title(Initialize)

@begin[lang=sh](code)
mkdir wallaby
cd wallaby
zick init
@end(code)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Install the Dependencies)

The tool is published as a zip with a @c(bin/wally) inside it:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k wally -V 2.1.0 \
    -l https://downloads.example.com/wally-2.1.0-linux-x64.zip
@end(code)

The data pack is versioned independently and takes a while to
download, so record it without fetching it for now:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k parks-data -V 2026.07 \
    -l https://data.example.com/parks-2026.07.zip -W
@end(code)

zick's store now has two packages; @c(wally) has its files, and
@c(parks-data) is recorded but not yet installed.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Install the Rest Later)

Later, when you have time for the download, complete the install by
re-adding the package with downloading enabled.  Because the package
is already recorded at this version, re-adding it counts as an
in-place replacement, which zick refuses by default — pass
@c(-i)/@c(--enable-allow-inplace) to allow it:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k parks-data -V 2026.07 \
    -l https://data.example.com/parks-2026.07.zip -i
@end(code)

The archive is fetched, unpacked, and the package's files are recorded.
If a package version ever needs to change, re-add it with the new
version and zick upgrades it in place, putting changed configuration
files aside rather than clobbering local edits.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Record What the Project Depends On)

Declare that @c(wally) and @c(parks-data) belong to the project itself
by installing a small source package that depends on both:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k wallaby -V 0.1.0 \
    -l https://downloads.example.com/wallaby-src.zip \
    -u wally -u parks-data -W
@end(code)

Now the dependency graph is explicit: @c(wally) and @c(parks-data)
have a depender, and the project's own package is its source.  That
makes lifecycle questions answerable:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick dependers -k wally
zick orphans
@end(code)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Verify Before You Trust the Tree)

After a checkout, a restore, or any operation that might have touched
the tree, check the whole install:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick verify -k wally
zick verify -k parks-data
@end(code)

Each exits 0 when the files on disk match the store.  When one does
not, it exits 4 and lists exactly which files are wrong and how
(@c(file-missing), @c(size-discrepancy), @c(checksum-discrepancy), and
so on) — the difference between "something might be off" and "this
file at this path changed".

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Remove a Dependency Cleanly)

When @c(wally) is no longer needed, remove it.  Nothing else depends on
it, so a plain removal works:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick remove -k wally
@end(code)

Removing @c(wallaby) itself cascades to the dependencies it declares,
so a full teardown is one command:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick remove -k wallaby -c
@end(code)

@end(section)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(In CI)

The same project in CI is a checkout plus the same commands — which is
the point: the install is scriptable and repeatable.

@begin[lang=sh](code)
git clone https://github.com/you/wallaby.git
cd wallaby

zick init
zick add -k wally -V 2.1.0 \
    -l https://downloads.example.com/wally-2.1.0-linux-x64.zip
zick add -k parks-data -V 2026.07 \
    -l https://data.example.com/parks-2026.07.zip
zick add -k wallaby -V 0.1.0 \
    -l https://downloads.example.com/wallaby-src.zip \
    -u wally -u parks-data -W
zick verify -k wally
zick verify -k parks-data
@end(code)

Because @c(verify) checks the on-disk files against recorded checksums,
this step fails the build when a download is corrupt, a file went
missing, or the environment drifted — with a precise report, not a
mysterious failure later.  Because the store lives in the project
(check it into git along with the sources if you like), the same
commands produce the same tree on every machine.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Using zick with the Resolver)

For a larger project the versions are chosen by a dependency resolver
rather than by hand.  zick pairs with @link[uri="https://github.com/djha-skin/dsolv"](dsolv),
the generic dependency resolver; the two are independent tools, built
to pair: dsolv reads the requirements, decides which versions a build
needs, and hands you the URLs of the artifacts; zick downloads those
exact archives, installs them, and verifies them.

The @link[uri="https://djha-skin.github.io/dsolv/a-longer-example.html"](dsolv manual) works through a concrete repository of five zip artifacts with
the dependency tree @c(a -> b), @c(b -> c), @c(b -> d), @c(d -> e), and
@c(c -> e) — artifacts @c(a@2.1.0), @c(b@2.3.0), @c(c@3.5.0),
@c(d@0.5.0), and @c(e@1.8.0) served at
@c(https://example.com/repo/<name>-<version>.zip).  Assume that very
scenario: the repository index is at
@c(https://example.com/repo/index.dsrepo) and the project needs
@c(a).  dsolv resolves the whole tree and, with @c(--list-strat lazy),
prints it in install order — dependencies before the packages that
need them:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
dsolv resolve-locations \
    --requirement a \
    --repository https://example.com/repo/index.dsrepo \
    --list-strat lazy \
    --output-format json
@end(code)

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{"result":"successful","packages":[
{"id":"e","version":"1.8.0","location":"https://example.com/repo/e-1.8.0.zip","requirements":[]},
{"id":"d","version":"0.5.0","location":"https://example.com/repo/d-0.5.0.zip","requirements":[...]},
{"id":"c","version":"3.5.0","location":"https://example.com/repo/c-3.5.0.zip","requirements":[...]},
{"id":"b","version":"2.3.0","location":"https://example.com/repo/b-2.3.0.zip","requirements":[...]},
{"id":"a","version":"2.1.0","location":"https://example.com/repo/a-2.1.0.zip","requirements":[...]}]}
@end(code)

Each package carries its @c(id), @c(version), and @c(location) —
exactly the three arguments @c(zick add) takes.  A short script pipes
the JSON through @c(jq) and installs each package in order.  Because
the resolver listed dependencies first, every @c(zick add) finds the
packages it needs already present:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

zick init

dsolv resolve-locations \
    --requirement a \
    --repository https://example.com/repo/index.dsrepo \
    --list-strat lazy \
    --output-format json \
| jq -r '.packages[] | [.id, .version, .location] | @tsv' \
| while IFS=$'\t' read -r id version location; do
    zick add -k "$id" -V "$version" -l "$location"
done

zick verify -k a
@end(code)

That stanza — a resolver deciding @i(what) and @i(in what order), zick
installing and verifying — is a complete CI dependency step: the same
commands produce the same tree on every machine, and @c(zick verify)
proves the resolved artifact tree is intact.

The division of labor keeps each tool small: the resolver has no
opinion about file layout — it returns locations — and zick has no
opinion about version selection — it takes the versions it is given.
Each can be used alone, and together they make a build step that
resolves, installs, and proves itself intact.

@end(section)

@end(section)
